  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "8-connected body-color objects on the background, each crossed by a rarer marker-color fracture line",
  "transform": "erase the marker pixels; in each wide object shift body pixels one cell left/right away from the row marker, and in each tall object shift body pixels one cell up/down away from the column marker"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 8-19 square [observed]
  background: 0
  colors: 2 foreground -- body (majority, 9 [observed]) and marker (minority, 6 [observed])
  objects:
    count: 1-7 per grid
    shape: solid filled rectangle (100% fill), non-square bbox
    dimensions: longer = 2 * shorter; shorter in 2-6, longer in 4-12
    orientation: wide (w > h) or tall (h > w); mix allowed
    fracture_line: |
      Across the shorter axis: 1 marker pixel per row (wide) or per col (tall).
      Marker offset along the longer axis uses exactly 2 adjacent values {S-1, S}
      (the two central indices, since L=2S). Each slice picks one; line is 8-connected.
    placement: |
      Expanded bboxes (original +1 on each shift-direction side) must fit in grid
      and not overlap. Min Chebyshev gap >=2 between original bboxes.
  transform: |
    Erase markers; shift body pixels 1 cell outward from fracture line
    (left/right for wide, up/down for tall). Output has body color only.
    A 3-cell-wide zigzag gap appears between the two split halves.

generation_strategy:
  approach: input-first (input is simpler -- solid rectangles with a zigzag seam)
  steps:
    1. Choose square grid size N in 8-19.
    2. Pick body and marker colors (2 distinct non-bg).
    3. Choose 1-7 objects. For each, pick S in 2-6, L = 2*S, and orientation.
    4. Place as solid filled rectangles on the grid with >=1 margin on each
       shift-direction side and >=2 Chebyshev gap between any two bboxes.
    5. Generate fracture line per object: for each of the S slices, assign
       marker offset S-1 or S (along longer axis), ensuring 8-connectivity.
       Replace those body pixels with marker color.
    6. Validate: marker count (=S) < body count (=S*(2S-1)), always true.
```
